  "summary": "Four preprints pair LLM components with explicit evidence retrieval or validation to improve accuracy and interpretability across diverse biological tasks.",
  "synthesis": "The preprints share a design pattern: a large language model is coupled to an external evidence source or a measurable criterion, and performance is assessed against that criterion. Spec2Function uses a dual-encoder spectrum-text model with retrieval augmentation to infer biological function from untargeted LC-MS/MS spectra. statLens, an LLM-code-human framework, improves accuracy and reproducibility of differential expression analysis across 102 tasks from 74 omics datasets. StoiAgent integrates sequence, structural and literature evidence to predict protein complex stoichiometry with 75% top-1 accuracy. The narrative-research tool introduces an LLM-derived contextual information gain metric that tracks human event-importance ratings and default mode network coactivation. The novelty lies in using language models as quantitative instruments whose outputs are checked against independent measures. This thread is dominant rather than universal, since the narrative study is a metric-validation effort rather than a prediction task.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
